RENAME(1)                    Funny CLI Manual                    RENAME(1)

NAME
    rename, ren — Rename a file or directory, because sometimes
                  "final_v2_REALLY_FINAL" needs a little work.

SYNOPSIS
    rename <old> <new>
    ren <old> <new>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'rename' command changes the name or path of an existing
    filesystem entry.

    It takes an old path and a new path, then asks the filesystem to
    perform the transformation.

    No copying occurs.

    No contents are changed.

    The thing simply wakes up one morning and discovers it has a
    different name.

ALIASES
    ren
        An alias for 'rename'.

        For users who believe four letters are already excessive.

ARGUMENTS
    old
        The existing file or directory to rename.

        This is who they used to be.

    new
        The new name or destination path.

        This is who they are now.

BEHAVIOR
    The old path is renamed to the new path using the filesystem's
    native rename operation.

    On success, the command prints:

        Renamed

    A brief but emotionally supportive confirmation.

ERRORS
    rename error: <error>
        Something prevented the rename.

        Possible causes include:
        - The old path does not exist.
        - Permission denied.
        - The destination is invalid.
        - The filesystem has objections to the proposed identity change.
        - The requested new name is already causing trouble.

USAGE ERRORS
    > rename old.txt

        Usage: rename <old> <new>

        You provided a past but neglected to provide a future.

    > ren draft.txt masterpiece.txt
        Renamed

        Same operation. Much less typing.

EXAMPLES
    > rename draft.txt final.txt
        Renamed

        It is now legally a final.txt.

    > rename final.txt final_FINAL.txt
        Renamed

        Progress.

    > rename final_FINAL.txt final_FINAL_v2.txt
        Renamed

        We all know where this is going.

    > ren old-name.txt new-name.txt
        Renamed

        The file has undergone a rebranding.

    > rename boring-project awesome-project
        Renamed

        The filesystem accepts your optimism.

FUN FACTS
    - 'rename' changes the name/path without manually copying the
      contents.
    - Files and directories can both be renamed.
    - 'ren' is provided for maximum keystroke efficiency.
    - The command does not ask for confirmation.
    - A rename is not therapy, but it can make a file feel new again.
    - The filesystem remembers what happened, even if your coworkers
      don't.

